  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "app/jobs/follow_ups/refresh_out_of_date_statuses_job.rb",
      "content": "When no follow-ups have an out-of-date status, `@follow_up_ids` is an empty array, yet the job still schedules `RefreshStatusesJob.perform_later([])` (a no-op job) and posts a misleading Slack message that claims it is refreshing statuses for no follow-ups. Consider early-returning when `@follow_up_ids.empty?`.",
      "suggestion_code": "    if @follow_up_ids.any?\n      notify_on_slack\n      FollowUps::RefreshStatusesJob.perform_later(@follow_up_ids)\n    end",
      "existing_code": "    notify_on_slack\n    FollowUps::RefreshStatusesJob.perform_later(@follow_up_ids)",
      "start_line": 8,
      "end_line": 9,
      "category": "performance",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/jobs/follow_ups/refresh_out_of_date_statuses_job.rb",
      "content": "This posts the complete list of follow-up IDs to the Slack notification channel. When many follow-ups are out of date, this can produce a very large message and expose a big payload. Prefer sending a count (e.g. `#{@follow_up_ids.size}`) or truncating the list instead of dumping every ID.",
      "suggestion_code": "    SlackClient.send_to_notif_channel(\n      \"✨ Rafraîchit les statuts pour: #{@follow_up_ids.size} follow-ups\"\n    )",
      "existing_code": "    SlackClient.send_to_notif_channel(\n      \"✨ Rafraîchit les statuts pour: #{@follow_up_ids}\"\n    )",
      "start_line": 15,
      "end_line": 17,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/models/sidekiq/scheduler.rb",
      "content": "`reschedule_existing_job` performs a second scan of the scheduled set via `existing_job` instead of reusing the record found in `schedule_uniq_job`. If the job leaves the scheduled set between the check and this call (e.g. it starts processing or is moved to the retry set), `existing_job` returns `nil` and `.reschedule` raises `NoMethodError`. Reuse the found record (and consider that the whole check-and-schedule sequence is not atomic, so a concurrent call could schedule a duplicate).",
      "suggestion_code": "  def reschedule_existing_job(job)\n    job.reschedule(@at) if job\n  end",
      "existing_code": "  def reschedule_existing_job\n    existing_job.reschedule(@at)\n  end",
      "start_line": 34,
      "end_line": 36,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/jobs/follow_ups/refresh_statuses_job.rb",
      "content": "`set_status` is invoked manually here and then again by the `before_save :set_status` callback when `save!` runs. `set_status` also calls `participations.reload` and `invitations.reload`, so each follow-up triggers redundant recomputation and extra queries (and the `:invitations, :rdvs` preloads are largely bypassed since `invitations.reload` re-fetches them). Call `save!` alone to let the `before_save` callback handle status computation, and drop the redundant manual invocation.",
      "suggestion_code": "      follow_up.save!",
      "existing_code": "      follow_up.set_status\n      follow_up.save!",
      "start_line": 5,
      "end_line": 6,
      "category": "performance",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/models/follow_up.rb",
      "content": "Changing participations from `dependent: :nullify` to `dependent: :destroy` is a significant behavioral change. When a follow_up is destroyed, each participation now triggers its `before_destroy :send_france_travail_delete_webhook` (sending Actually/France Travail cancellation webhooks) and its `after_commit :refresh_follow_up_status`. In the current codebase, `FollowUps::CleanUnused` only destroys `not_invited` follow_ups (which have no participations), but this now recursively destroys participation history (and its notifications via `dependent: :destroy`) and enqueues cancellation webhooks for any follow_up destruction path with existing participations (e.g. user RGPD cleanup). Please confirm the France Travail cancel cascade and the participation data loss are intended; if not, prefer keeping `:nullify` or scoping the destroy behavior.",
      "existing_code": "  has_many :participations, dependent: :destroy",
      "start_line": 10,
      "end_line": 10,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/models/follow_up.rb",
      "content": "`refresh_status_at` only returns a single timestamp (the later of last invitation expiry and last rdv start). For a follow_up that both has an upcoming RDV and a later-expiring invitation, only one refresh is scheduled at the later event. The RDV-based transition (e.g. `rdv_pending -> rdv_needs_status_update` right after the RDV) won't be scheduled at RDV time and will be deferred until the (potentially much later) invitation expiry, leaving the status stale until the daily `RefreshOutOfDateStatusesJob` runs. Consider scheduling a refresh per pending event rather than a single max date, or explicitly documenting that the CRON is the intended backstop for these cases.",
      "existing_code": "  def refresh_status_at\n    start_from = [last_invitation_expires_at, last_rdv_starts_at].compact.max\n    start_from + 1.second if start_from\n  end",
      "start_line": 82,
      "end_line": 85,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/models/concerns/invitable.rb",
      "content": "`max_by(&:expires_at)` will raise when a follow_up has a mix of never-expiring invitations (expires_at == nil) and expiring ones. Enumerable#max_by compares the yielded values with `<=>`; comparing a Time with nil returns nil and then `nil > max` raises `ArgumentError: comparison of Time with nil failed` / NoMethodError. Given that invitations can be created with nil expires_at (organisations with `invitations_never_expire?`), a follow_up receiving invitations from different configurations can hit this and crash the scheduling job. Consider filtering nil expires_at first, e.g. `invitations.max_by { |i| i.expires_at.to_i }&.expires_at` or comparing only non-nil values.",
      "suggestion_code": "  def last_invitation_expires_at\n    invitations.filter_map(&:expires_at).max\n  end",
      "existing_code": "  def last_invitation_expires_at\n    invitations.max_by(&:expires_at)&.expires_at\n  end",
      "start_line": 51,
      "end_line": 53,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/models/sidekiq/scheduler.rb",
      "content": "`schedule_uniq_job` runs `scheduled_set.find` (which iterates the entire Sidekiq scheduled set) on every invocation, and it is called twice when an existing job is found (once in `existing_job`, once inside `reschedule_existing_job`). Since `PlanStatusRefreshJob` is enqueued on every invitation/participation create/update, this O(n) scan over all scheduled jobs happens very frequently and may become a bottleneck as the scheduled set grows. Consider memoizing the looked-up job and reusing it (and/or using a Redis SETNX-style dedup keyed by job class + args) instead of scanning the full set twice.",
      "suggestion_code": "  def schedule_uniq_job\n    if (job = existing_job)\n      job.reschedule(@at)\n    else\n      @job_class.set(wait_until: @at, queue: @queue).perform_later(*@args)\n    end\n  end",
      "existing_code": "  def schedule_uniq_job\n    if existing_job\n      reschedule_existing_job\n    else\n      @job_class.set(wait_until: @at, queue: @queue).perform_later(*@args)\n    end\n  end",
      "start_line": 13,
      "end_line": 19,
      "category": "performance",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "spec/support/sidekiq_helper.rb",
      "content": "Flushing the entire default Redis DB (`Sidekiq.redis(&:flushdb)` targets the app-wide `redis://localhost:6379` DB 0) is destructive in this project's CI: unit specs are executed via `parallel:spec` against a single shared Redis instance (see .github/workflows/ci.yml). One process running `with_sidekiq_enabled` will wipe the Sidekiq scheduled jobs/data that other concurrently-running processes have pushed, causing flaky/failing parallel specs. `flushdb` also clears every non-Sidekiq key on the DB. Consider isolating Sidekiq test data (e.g. a dedicated Redis DB/namespace for the test env, or scoping cleanup to keys with the Sidekiq prefix instead of a blanket flushdb).",
      "suggestion_code": "      Sidekiq.redis do |conn|\n        conn.scan_each(match: \"#{Sidekiq::TEST_KEY_PREFIX}*\") { |key| conn.del(key) }\n      end if Sidekiq::TEST_KEY_PREFIX",
      "existing_code": "      Sidekiq.redis(&:flushdb)",
      "start_line": 7,
      "end_line": 7,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "high"
    }
  ],
